// Headline flexibility case: an input field and an output field that share
// a name (`verdict`) are DISTINCT fields once nested, addressable as
// `input.verdict` and `output.verdict`.
//
// The Review action's input carries the *requested* verdict and its output
// carries the *system's* verdict. A Publish is permitted only if there was
// formerly a Review response where the requested verdict was "approve"
// AND the system's verdict was "deny" — i.e. a request/outcome mismatch.
// This predicate is only expressible because the two `verdict` fields are
// kept distinct; a flat model would have collapsed them into one key and
// this filter could not be written.
//
// Positive/negative control across two traces:
//   trace_1: Review response input.verdict="approve", output.verdict="deny"
//            → mismatch → Publish permitted.
//   trace_2: IDENTICAL except output.verdict="approve" (they agree) → the
//            `output.verdict: "deny"` constraint fails → NOT permitted.
// If input.verdict and output.verdict were the same key, trace_2 could not
// differ from trace_1 in the way that flips the verdict.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Publish",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h
        Drupe::Action::"Review"::response{ input.verdict: "approve", output.verdict: "deny" }
};
